    "nge fault. For two elements of one `Vec` or\n    slice, use `Vec::swap` or `slice_swap`, which check "
    "the bounds and allow `i == j`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::core\u{1f}replace\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn replace<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v)"
    " : T\u{1f}Puts `v` in `*r` and returns what was there.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn replace<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v) : "
    "T\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Puts `v` in `*r` and returns what was there.\n\n    `swap` with a fresh valu"
    "e. `overwrite` destroys the old value instead of returning it.\n\n    Example, \"replace: the old value"
    " comes back\":\n        i32 a = 1;\n        i32 old = replace(&mut a, 2);\n        assert(old == 1 && a "
    "== 2);\n        i32 b = 3;\n        swap(&mut a, &mut b);\n        assert(a == 3 && b == 2);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::cor"
    "e\u{1f}overwrite\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn overwrite<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v)\u{1f}Puts `v` in `*r` and destroys what w"
    "as there, at the call.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn overwrite<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v)\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Put"
    "s `v` in `*r` and destroys what was there, at the call.\n\n    The way to assign over a place that hol"
    "ds a resource, which plain assignment refuses.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct "
    "Vec<T>\u{1f}A growable array of `T`, owning its elements.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Vec<T>\n    in `std::col"
    "lections`\n\n    A growable array of `T`, owning its elements.\n\n    `v[i]` reads or writes element `i`"
    " (`index_out_of_bounds` past the end), `&v[lo..hi]` is a slice of it,\n    and `foreach` visits it by"
    " value, `&v` or `&mut v`. A reference to an element is stale after a `push`\n    that reallocates. Dr"
    "opping a `Vec` destroys its elements, first to last, then frees its buffer.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::"
    "new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::new<T>() : Vec<T>\u{1f}A new, empty `Vec`; nothing is allocated until it first grow"
    "s.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::new<T>() : Vec<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A new, empty `Vec`; nothing is a"
    "llocated until it first grows.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::len\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::len<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shar"
    "ed> v) : usize\u{1f}The number of elements in `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::len<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v) : usize\n"
    "    in `std::collections`\n\n    The number of elements in `v`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::push\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export"
    " fn Vec::push<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, T x)\u{1f}Appends `x` to the end of `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::push<"
    "T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, T x)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Appends `x` to the end of `v`.\n\n   "
    " Amortised constant time: when the buffer is full it is reallocated at twice its capacity, and refer"
    "ences\n    to elements held across that are stale at their next use. Faults (`alloc_failure`) when me"
    "mory runs out.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::pop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::pop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v) : Opti"
    "on<T>\u{1f}Removes the last element of `v` and returns it; `None` when `v` is empty.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::pop<"
    "T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v) : Option<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Removes the last element of "
    "`v` and returns it; `None` when `v` is empty.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::index_shared\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec:"
    ":index_shared<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, usize i) : ref<T, shared>\u{1f}The element at index `i` of `v`, a"
    "s a shared reference; what `v[i]` reads, so programs write `v[i]`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_shared<T>(r"
    "ef<Vec<T>, shared> v, usize i) : ref<T, shared>\n    in `std::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v"
    ".len, index_out_of_bounds\n\n    The element at index `i` of `v`, as a shared reference; what `v[i]` r"
    "eads, so programs write `v[i]`.\n\n    Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when `i >= len`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collecti"
    "ons\u{1f}Vec::index_exclusive\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_exclusive<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : r"
    "ef<T, exclusive>\u{1f}The element at index `i` of `v`, as an exclusive reference; what `v[i]` is when it "
    "is written, so programs write `v[i]`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_exclusive<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, u"
    "size i) : ref<T, exclusive>\n    in `std::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v.len, index_out_of_b"
    "ounds\n\n    The element at index `i` of `v`, as an exclusive reference; what `v[i]` is when it is wri"
    "tten, so\n    programs write `v[i]`.\n\n    Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when `i >= len`. A live slic"
    "e of `v` holding `i` conflicts with it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::reserve\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::reserve<T>"
    "(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize n)\u{1f}Makes room for `n` more elements: afterwards the capacity is at "
    "least `len + n`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::reserve<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize n)\n    in `std::collecti"
    "ons`\n\n    Makes room for `n` more elements: afterwards the capacity is at least `len + n`.\n\n    Grow"
    "s at most once, to the larger of `len + n` and twice the capacity, so small reserves in a loop stay\n"
    "    amortised; never shrinks and never changes the length or the elements. It changes only how fast "
    "a\n    program runs. `len + n` overflowing is `diag.arith-overflow`, before anything is allocated.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}"
    "std::collections\u{1f}Vec::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::drop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Destroys every ele"
    "ment of `self`, first to last, then frees the buffer; the destructor run when a `Vec` goes out of sc"
    "ope, not called by programs.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::drop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::colle"
    "ctions`\n\n    Destroys every element of `self`, first to last, then frees the buffer; the destructor "
    "run when a `Vec`\n    goes out of scope, not called by programs.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::sort_by\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn Vec::sort_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, fn(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less)\u{1f}S"
    "orts `v` by `less`, which is true when its first argument goes before its second.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::so"
    "rt_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, fn(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less)\n    in `std::coll"
    "ections`\n\n    Sorts `v` by `less`, which is true when its first argument goes before its second.\n\n  "
    "  Stable: equal elements (neither before the other) keep their order. Each element moves once.\n    `"
    "less` must be a strict weak order (never `less(a, a)`, transitive); with one that is not, the elemen"
    "ts\n    end as a permutation of what they were, in an unspecified order. For a key type `Vec::sort` n"
    "eeds no\n    `less`.\n\n    Example, \"Vec::sort_by: stable\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n        V"
    "ec::push(&mut v, 13);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 21);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 12);\n        Vec::pus"
    "h(&mut v, 25);\n        Vec::sort_by(&mut v, [](ref<i32, shared> a, ref<i32, shared> b) : bool { *a /"
    " 10 < *b / 10 });\n        assert(v[0] == 13 && v[1] == 12 && v[2] == 21 && v[3] == 25);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::colle"
    "ctions\u{1f}Vec::sort\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::sort<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\u{1f}Sorts `v` in its elements' own "
    "order, keeping equal elements in their order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::sort<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\n    "
    "in `std::collections`\n\n    Sorts `v` in its elements' own order, keeping equal elements in their ord"
    "er.\n\n    `T` must be a key type: integers by value, `false` before `true`, `str` and `String` by the"
    "ir bytes (a\n    prefix first), a struct field by field, an enum by the order its variants are declar"
    "ed in, then by\n    payload. `f32` and `f64` sort too (`[Sort-Float]`): by value, `-0.0` and `0.0` as"
    " equal, every NaN\n    after every other value. Any other `T` is rejected at compile time; use `Vec::"
    "sort_by` for those.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::binary_search_by\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search_by<T>(r"
    "ef<Vec<T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared> key, fn(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less) : Result<u"
    "size, usize>\u{1f}Searches `v`, sorted by `less`, for `key`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search_by<T>(ref<Vec<"
    "T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared> key, fn(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less) : Result<usize, u"
    "size>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Searches `v`, sorted by `less`, for `key`.\n\n    Returns `Ok(i)`"
    " with `i` the first position whose element is equal to `key` (neither goes before the\n    other), or"
    " `Err(i)` with `i` the position where `key` would be inserted to keep `v` sorted. `v` must be\n    so"
    "rted by the same `less`, or the result is unspecified.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::binary_search\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expo"
    "rt fn Vec::binary_search<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, probe<T> key) : Result<usize, usize>\u{1f}Searches `v`"
    ", sorted in its elements' own order, for `key`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared>"
    " v, probe<T> key) : Result<usize, usize>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Searches `v`, sorted in its "
    "elements' own order, for `key`.\n\n    `T` a key type or a float, as for `Vec::sort`. `Ok(i)` for the "
    "first position holding an element equal to `key`,\n    otherwise `Err(i)`, the position where `key` w"
    "ould go.\n\n    Example, \"Vec::binary_search: found and missing\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n   "
    "     Vec::push(&mut v, 10);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 20);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 20);\n        Ve"
    "c::push(&mut v, 30);\n        i32 present = 20;\n        i32 absent = 25;\n        assert(Result::unwra"
    "p_or(Vec::binary_search(&v, present), 99) == 1);\n        match (Vec::binary_search(&v, absent))\n    "
    "    {\n            Ok(_)  : assert(false),\n            Err(i) : assert(i == 3),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::coll"
    "ections\u{1f}Vec::clear\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::clear<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\u{1f}Removes every element of `v`"
    ", destroying them first to last; the buffer is kept for reuse.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::clear<T>(ref<Vec<T>, "
    "exclusive> v)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Removes every element of `v`, destroying them first to "
    "last; the buffer is kept for reuse.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::insert\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::insert<T>(ref<V"
    "ec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i, T x)\u{1f}Inserts `x` at index `i`, moving the elements from `i` on up by o"
    "ne.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::insert<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i, T x)\n    in `std::collections`\n    "
    "requires\n        i <= v.len, index_out_of_bounds\n\n    Inserts `x` at index `i`, moving the elements "
    "from `i` on up by one.\n\n    `i` may equal `len`, which appends. Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when "
    "`i > len`. Linear in the number\n    of elements after `i`; no element is copied.\n\n    Example, \"Vec:"
    ":insert: middle and end\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 1);\n        Vec"
    "::push(&mut v, 3);\n        Vec::insert(&mut v, 1, 2);\n        Vec::insert(&mut v, 3, 4);\n        ass"
    "ert(Vec::len(&v) == 4);\n        assert(v[0] == 1 && v[1] == 2 && v[2] == 3 && v[3] == 4);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::col"
    "lections\u{1f}Vec::remove\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::remove<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : T\u{1f}Removes the "
    "element at index `i` and returns it, moving the elements after it down by one.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::remov"
    "e<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : T\n    in `std::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v.len"
    ", index_out_of_bounds\n\n    Removes the element at index `i` and returns it, moving the elements afte"
    "r it down by one.\n\n    Keeps the order and takes time linear in the elements after `i`; `Vec::swap_r"
    "emove` takes constant time\n    but does not keep the order. Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when `i >"
    "= len`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::swap_remove\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::swap_remove<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> "
    "v, usize i) : T\u{1f}Removes the element at index `i` and returns it, moving the last element into its pl"
    "ace.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::swap_remove<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : T\n    in `std::collections`"
    "\n    requires\n        i < v.len, index_out_of_bounds\n\n    Removes the element at index `i` and retur"
    "ns it, moving the last element into its place.\n\n    Constant time, but the order is not kept; `Vec::"
    "remove` keeps it. Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when\n    `i >= len`.\n\n    Example, \"Vec::swap_remov"
    "e: order not kept\":\n        Vec<i32> a = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut a, 1);\n        Vec::push"
    "(&mut a, 2);\n        Vec::push(&mut a, 3);\n        Vec::push(&mut a, 4);\n        Vec<i32> b = Vec::c"
    "lone(&a);\n        assert(Vec::remove(&mut a, 0) == 1);\n        assert(a[0] == 2 && a[1] == 3 && a[2]"
    " == 4);\n        assert(Vec::swap_remove(&mut b, 0) == 1);\n        assert(b[0] == 4 && b[1] == 2 && b"
    "[2] == 3);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::clone<T: clone>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v) : "
    "Vec<T>\u{1f}A new `Vec` holding a copy of each element of `v`, in order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::clone<T: clone>("
    "ref<Vec<T>, shared> v) : Vec<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A new `Vec` holding a copy of each el"
    "ement of `v`, in order.\n\n    `T` must be `clone` (owning nothing); for other element types use `Vec:"
    ":clone_by`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::from_slice\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::from_slice<T: clone>(slice<T, share"
    "d> s) : Vec<T>\u{1f}A new `Vec` holding a copy of each element of `s`, in order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::from_sli"
    "ce<T: clone>(slice<T, shared> s) : Vec<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A new `Vec` holding a copy "
    "of each element of `s`, in order.\n\n    `T` must be `clone`. The buffer is allocated once, at the sli"
    "ce's length.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::truncate\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::truncate<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v"
    ", usize n)\u{1f}Keeps the first `n` elements of `v` and destroys the rest, last first.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::tr"
    "uncate<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize n)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Keeps the first `n` elem"
    "ents of `v` and destroys the rest, last first.\n\n    `n` at or beyond the length leaves `v` unchanged"
    ".\n\n    Example, \"Vec::truncate: beyond the end\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::filled(3, 7);\n        Vec"
    "::truncate(&mut v, 10);\n        assert(Vec::len(&v) == 3);\n        Vec::truncate(&mut v, 1);\n       "
    " assert(Vec::len(&v) == 1);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::clone_by\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::clone_by<T>(ref<Vec<T"
    ">, shared> v, fn(ref<T, shared>) : T copy) : Vec<T>\u{1f}A new `Vec` holding `copy(&x)` for each element "
    "`x` of `v`, in order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::clone_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, fn(ref<T, shared>) : T copy"
    ") : Vec<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A new `Vec` holding `copy(&x)` for each element `x` of `v`"
