    "n\u{1f}export fn Vec::contains<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, probe<T> x) : bool\u{1f}Whether `v` has an elemen"
    "t equal to what `x` probes (as for `Vec::index_of`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::contains<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, sha"
    "red> v, probe<T> x) : bool\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Whether `v` has an element equal to what `"
    "x` probes (as for `Vec::index_of`).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::contains_ref\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::contains_"
    "ref<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared> x) : bool\u{1f}Whether `v` has an element equal to `*x`,"
    " given a reference to an element rather than its probe (as `HashMap::contains_ref`, for generic code"
    ": `fn unique<T: eq>(…)`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::contains_ref<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared"
    "> x) : bool\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Whether `v` has an element equal to `*x`, given a referen"
    "ce to an element rather than its probe (as\n    `HashMap::contains_ref`, for generic code: `fn unique"
    "<T: eq>(…)`).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::eq\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::eq<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> a, ref<Ve"
    "c<T>, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` and `b` have the same length and equal elements in the same orde"
    "r.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::eq<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> a, ref<Vec<T>, shared> b) : bool\n    in `std::colle"
    "ctions`\n\n    Whether `a` and `b` have the same length and equal elements in the same order.\n\n    Exa"
    "mple, \"Vec::eq: length and order\":\n        Vec<u8> a = Vec::from_slice(&[1: u8, 2, 3][0..3]);\n      "
    "  Vec<u8> b = Vec::from_slice(&[1: u8, 2, 3][0..3]);\n        Vec<u8> c = Vec::from_slice(&[1: u8, 3,"
    " 2][0..3]);\n        Vec<u8> d = Vec::from_slice(&[1: u8, 2][0..2]);\n        assert(Vec::eq(&a, &b));"
    "\n        assert(!Vec::eq(&a, &c));\n        assert(!Vec::eq(&a, &d));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::reverse"
    "\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::reverse<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\u{1f}Reverses the order of the elements of `v` in"
    " place; nothing is copied or destroyed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::reverse<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\n    in "
    "`std::collections`\n\n    Reverses the order of the elements of `v` in place; nothing is copied or des"
    "troyed.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::retain\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::retain<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, fn(ref<"
    "T, shared>) : bool keep)\u{1f}Keeps the elements for which `keep` returns true, in their order, and destr"
    "oys the rest.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::retain<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, fn(ref<T, shared>) : bool keep)\n  "
    "  in `std::collections`\n\n    Keeps the elements for which `keep` returns true, in their order, and d"
    "estroys the rest.\n\n    `keep` is called once per element, in index order, before any element is dest"
    "royed. The kept elements\n    are moved down by `Vec::swap`, not copied, which leaves the removed one"
    "s at the end in an order of the\n    swaps' making, not their original one; they are then destroyed a"
    "s `Vec::truncate` destroys, from the\n    last place. A program whose destructors must run in a set o"
    "rder removes the elements itself.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::dedup\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::dedup<T: eq>(ref<V"
    "ec<T>, exclusive> v)\u{1f}Removes every element equal to the one kept before it, keeping the first of eac"
    "h run.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::dedup<T: eq>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Removes"
    " every element equal to the one kept before it, keeping the first of each run.\n\n    Only adjacent du"
    "plicates are removed, so a sorted `Vec` is left with one of each value. The removed\n    elements are"
    " destroyed as `Vec::retain` destroys its removed ones, in an order of their own.\n\n    Example, \"Vec:"
    ":dedup: adjacent only\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 1);\n        Vec::"
    "push(&mut v, 1);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 2);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 1);\n        Vec::dedup(&mut"
    " v);\n        assert(Vec::len(&v) == 3);\n        assert(v[0] == 1 && v[1] == 2 && v[2] == 1);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::"
    "collections\u{1f}Vec::swap\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::swap<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i, usize j)\u{1f}Exchange"
    "s elements `i` and `j` of `v`; nothing happens when `i == j`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::swap<T>(ref<Vec<T>, ex"
    "clusive> v, usize i, usize j)\n    in `std::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v.len && j < v.len,"
    " index_out_of_bounds\n\n    Exchanges elements `i` and `j` of `v`; nothing happens when `i == j`.\n\n   "
    " Use it rather than `swap(&mut v[i], &mut v[j])`, which is two exclusive borrows of one place when\n "
    "   `i == j`. Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when either index is `>= len`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}HashMa"
    "p\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct HashMap<K, V>\u{1f}A hash table mapping keys of type `K` to values of type `V`, ke"
    "pt in insertion order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct HashMap<K, V>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A hash table mapp"
    "ing keys of type `K` to values of type `V`, kept in insertion order.\n\n    `K` must be a key type: an"
