    "\n    in `std::text`\n    removed: write a == t (D-0308)\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}String::view\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn String:"
    ":view\u{1f}\u{1f}removed\u{1f}export fn String::view\n    in `std::text`\n    removed: write &s[lo..hi] (D-0308)\n\u{1f}\u{1e}st"
    "d::text\u{1f}StringView::sub\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn StringView::sub\u{1f}\u{1f}removed\u{1f}export fn StringView::sub\n    in `std:"
    ":text`\n    removed: write &v[lo..hi] (D-0308)\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}stdout_write\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export unsafe extern "
    "fn stdout_write(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize\u{1f}Writes the `len` bytes at `buf` to standard outpu"
    "t: the primitive under `printf`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn stdout_write(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : "
    "isize\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Writes the `len` bytes at `buf` to standard output: the primitive under "
    "`printf`.\n\n    Called inside `unsafe`, with `buf` pointing at `len` readable bytes; its result is a "
    "claim like any\n    extern's. What it writes goes through the same buffer as `printf` (`flush_stdout`"
    "). Programs write with\n    `printf`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}stdin_read\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn stdin_re"
    "ad(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize\u{1f}Reads up to `len` bytes of standard input into `buf`: the prim"
    "itive under `read_all_bytes`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn stdin_read(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize"
    "\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Reads up to `len` bytes of standard input into `buf`: the primitive under `re"
    "ad_all_bytes`.\n\n    Called inside `unsafe`, with `buf` pointing at `len` writable bytes. Its result,"
    " a claim like any\n    extern's, is the number of bytes read: 0 at the end of the input, negative whe"
    "n the input cannot be read.\n    Programs read with `read_line`, `read_all` or `read_all_bytes`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}st"
    "d::io\u{1f}flush_stdout\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn flush_stdout()\u{1f}Writes out what standard output still holds in its bu"
    "ffer.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn flush_stdout()\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Writes out what standard output still holds in"
    " its buffer.\n\n    Standard output is buffered; standard error is not. Reading standard input, writin"
    "g to standard error,\n    `exit` and the program's end write the buffer out themselves, so this is ne"
    "eded only when standard output\n    is a pipe or a file that someone watches while the program runs ("
    "progress, a log), and before handing over\n    to C code that writes through `stdio`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}read_"
    "line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_line() : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of standard input, w"
    "ithout its line ending, or `Ok(None)` at the end of the input.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_line() : Result<Optio"
    "n<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::io`\n\n    The next line of standard input, without its line ending"
    ", or `Ok(None)` at the end of the input.\n\n    - A line is every byte up to the next `\\n`, which is c"
    "onsumed and not included; a `\\r` just before it is\n      consumed with it, so a Windows line ending "
    "reads as a Unix one. A last line with no `\\n` is still a\n      line, and keeps a final `\\r`.\n    - `"
    "Ok(None)` when the input ended before the line's first byte, and on every call after that.\n    - `Er"
    "r(FileError::Utf8(e))` when the line is not UTF-8, `e.offset` counted within the line; the line is\n "
    "     consumed, so the next call reads the one after it.\n    - `Err(FileError::Io)` when standard inp"
    "ut cannot be read (a directory given as standard input among such\n      cases: the reason is not tol"
    "d apart); the bytes of the line read so far are lost.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}read_all_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_all"
    "_bytes() : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\u{1f}The rest of standard input, as bytes; empty when the input has"
    " already ended.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_all_bytes() : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\n    in `std::io`\n\n    The r"
    "est of standard input, as bytes; empty when the input has already ended.\n\n    `Err(FileError::Io)` w"
    "hen standard input cannot be read. `read_all` takes it as text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}read_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read"
    "_all() : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}The rest of standard input, as text.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_all() : Resu"
    "lt<String, FileError>\n    in `std::io`\n\n    The rest of standard input, as text.\n\n    `Err(FileError"
    "::Utf8(e))` when it is not UTF-8, and `Err(FileError::Io)` when it cannot be read;\n    `read_all_byt"
    "es` takes it as bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileError\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum FileError\u{1f}The error of every input and o"
    "utput operation of `std`, for files and standard input alike.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum FileError\n    in `std::fs"
    "`\n\n    The error of every input and output operation of `std`, for files and standard input alike.\n\n"
    "    - `NotFound`: no such file, or a directory on the path does not exist.\n    - `Denied`: the envir"
    "onment refuses the operation.\n    - `IsDirectory`: the path names a directory where a file is needed"
    ".\n    - `NotDirectory`: a part of the path that must be a directory is not one.\n    - `NoSpace`: the"
    " device (or the user's quota on it) is full.\n    - `TooManyOpen`: the program, or the system, has as"
    " many files open as it may.\n    - `NotEmpty`: a directory to remove still holds entries.\n    - `Link"
    "Loop`: a chain of symbolic links on the path is too long, or loops back on itself.\n    - `SameFile`:"
    " a copy's target is its source (the same name written another way, or a link to it).\n    - `CrossDev"
    "ice`: a rename to another file system, which `rename` cannot do (copy, then remove).\n    - `NameTooL"
    "ong`: a name on the path, or the path, is longer than the system allows.\n    - `InvalidName`: the sy"
    "stem refuses the name or the request as such (a name holding NUL, a directory\n      renamed into its"
    "elf).\n    - `NotOpenForThat`: the file is not open for the operation (a write to a `File` from `File"
    "::open`).\n    - `ArgsTooLong`: a `Command`'s arguments and environment are more than the system take"
    "s.\n    - `Io`: any other failure (a device error, …).\n    - `Utf8(e)`: the bytes read are not UTF-"
    "8; `e` gives the offset of the first byte that is not.\n\n    Variants:\n        NotFound\n        Denie"
    "d\n        Io\n        Utf8(Utf8Error)\n        IsDirectory\n        NotDirectory\n        NoSpace\n      "
    "  TooManyOpen\n        NotEmpty\n        LinkLoop\n        SameFile\n        CrossDevice\n        NameToo"
    "Long\n        InvalidName\n        NotOpenForThat\n        ArgsTooLong\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileError::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn FileError::text(ref<FileError, shared> e) : str\u{1f}The error as a short phrase for a person (`no"
    "t found`, `permission denied`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn FileError::text(ref<FileError, shared> e) : str\n    in `s"
    "td::fs`\n\n    The error as a short phrase for a person (`not found`, `permission denied`).\n\n    Examp"
    "le, \"FileError::text: a phrase to print\":\n        FileError e = FileError::NotFound;\n        assert("
    "FileError::text(&e) == \"not found\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}read_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_file(StringView path) : R"
    "esult<String, FileError>\u{1f}The whole contents of the file at `path`, as text.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_file(Str"
    "ingView path) : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The whole contents of the file at `p"
    "ath`, as text.\n\n    A relative path is resolved against the working directory. `Err(Utf8(e))` when t"
    "he bytes are not UTF-8,\n    `e` giving the offset of the first that is not; `Err(NotFound)` when no "
    "such file exists; `Err(Denied)`\n    when the environment refuses; `Err(IsDirectory)` for a directory"
    "; `Err(Io)` for another failure. Use\n    `read_bytes` for bytes that need not be text, and `File` fo"
    "r a file too large to hold.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}write_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn write_file(StringView path, StringView"
    " text) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `text`, creating it or replac"
    "ing its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn write_file(StringView path, StringView text) : Result<void, FileError>\n"
    "    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `text`, creating it or replacing its con"
    "tents.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when a directory on the path does not exist; `Err(Denied)` when the envi"
    "ronment refuses\n    the write; `Err(Io)` for any other failure.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resourc"
    "e struct File\u{1f}An open file, read and written in order from a position that `seek` moves.\u{1f}\u{1f}export res"
    "ource struct File\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    An open file, read and written in order from a position that"
    " `seek` moves.\n\n    Every `File` comes from `File::open`, `create`, `append`, `open_rw` or `create_n"
    "ew`. Positions are `u64`,\n    so a file larger than the address space is still addressed. Reading an"
    "d writing are buffered; the bytes\n    written reach the file no later than the next operation on it,"
    " any other file-system operation of the\n    program, the start of a child process, or the program's "
    "end.\n\n    Destroying a `File` closes it without making it durable and without reporting a failure; a"
    " program\n    that must know its bytes were stored calls `File::close`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::open\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expor"
    "t fn File::open(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\u{1f}Opens the existing file at `path` for rea"
    "ding, at position 0.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::open(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\n    in `std::f"
    "s`\n\n    Opens the existing file at `path` for reading, at position 0.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when ther"
    "e is no such file; `Err(Denied)` when the environment refuses; `Err(IsDirectory)`\n    when the path "
    "names a directory; the other variants of `FileError` for the failures they name.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::cre"
    "ate\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::create(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` fo"
    "r writing from empty, creating it or discarding its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::create(StringView pat"
    "h) : Result<File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the file at `path` for writing from empty, "
    "creating it or discarding its contents.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when a directory on the path does not e"
    "xist; `Err(Denied)`, `Err(Io)` as `File::open`'s.\n    Use `File::create_new` when an existing file m"
    "ust not be overwritten.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::append\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::append(StringView path) : Result<F"
    "ile, FileError>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` for writing at its end, creating it if it does not exist.\u{1f}\u{1f}"
    "export fn File::append(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the fi"
    "le at `path` for writing at its end, creating it if it does not exist.\n\n    Every write lands at the"
    " end of the file.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::open_rw\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::open_rw(StringView path) : Result<File,"
    " FileError>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` for reading and writing, creating it if it does not exist, its "
    "contents kept.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::open_rw(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n"
    "\n    Opens the file at `path` for reading and writing, creating it if it does not exist, its content"
    "s kept.\n\n    The position starts at 0; a write lands at the position reading has reached.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}"
    "File::read\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) :"
    " Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends up to `max` bytes from the file's position to the end of `buf` and"
    " returns how many.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize "
    "max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Appends up to `max` bytes from the file's posi"
    "tion to the end of `buf` and returns how many.\n\n    At most a MiB is read at a time, whatever `max` "
    "is; fewer bytes come only at the end of the file, and\n    `Ok(0)` (for a `max` above 0) means the en"
    "d. `Err(NotOpenForThat)` on a file opened only for writing\n    (`Err(Denied)` on Windows).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs"
    "\u{1f}File::read_to_end\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read_to_end(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> bu"
    "f) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends the rest of the file, from its position, to the end of `buf` a"
    "nd returns how many bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read_to_end(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusi"
    "ve> buf) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Appends the rest of the file, from its pos"
    "ition, to the end of `buf` and returns how many bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::read_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::"
    "read_line(ref<File, exclusive> f) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the file, wit"
    "hout its final `'\\n'` or one `'\\r'` just before it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read_line(ref<File, exclusive> "
    "f) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The next line of the file, without its "
    "final `'\\n'` or one `'\\r'` just before it.\n\n    `Ok(None)` at the end of the file. A last line with "
    "no `'\\n'` is still a line. `Err(Utf8(e))` when the\n    line is not UTF-8, `e`'s offset counted from "
    "the line's start; `Err(NotOpenForThat)` on a file opened\n    only for writing (`Err(Denied)` on Wind"
    "ows). Lines end as `read_line` on standard input ends them. A\n    program that must know whether the"
    " line ended (one following a file still being written) reads it with\n    `File::read_line_with_end`."
    "\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::read_line_with_end\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read_line_with_end(ref<File, exclusive> f) : "
    "Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the file with its line end kept: it ends with `'\\"
    "n'` unless the file ends first.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read_line_with_end(ref<File, exclusive> f) : Result"
    "<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The next line of the file with its line end kept: "
    "it ends with `'\\n'` unless the file ends first.\n\n    For a program that follows a file another one i"
    "s still writing (a log), or reads a line-oriented\n    protocol: a line that does not end with `'\\n'`"
    " is the last one so far, cut short, and its position can be\n    returned to with `File::seek` to rea"
    "d it again whole. `'\\r'` is kept too. Otherwise as `File::read_line`.\n\n    Example, \"File::read_line"
    "_with_end: a line cut short\":\n        String path = path_join(&temp_dir(), &sprintf(\"cb-line-end-%v."
    "txt\", process_id()));\n        _ = write_file(&path, \"first\\r\\npart\");\n        File f = Result::unwra"
    "p(File::open(&path));\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Result::unwrap(File::read_line_with_end(&mut f))"
    ") == \"first\\r\\n\");\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Result::unwrap(File::read_line_with_end(&mut f))) ="
    "= \"part\");\n        assert(Option::is_none(&Result::unwrap(File::read_line_with_end(&mut f))));\n     "
    "   _ = File::close(f);\n        _ = remove_file(&path);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::write\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::writ"
    "e(ref<File, exclusive> f, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Writes all of `data` at "
    "the file's position, or at its end for a file opened by `File::append`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::write(ref<F"
    "ile, exclusive> f, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Writes al"
    "l of `data` at the file's position, or at its end for a file opened by `File::append`.\n\n    The file"
    " grows as needed and the position moves past the bytes written. Writes are buffered (see `File`);\n  "
    "  a failure the environment reports late is reported by the file's next operation. `Err(NotOpenForTh"
    "at)` on\n    a file opened only for reading (`Err(Denied)` on Windows).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::write_text\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn File::write_text(ref<File, exclusive> f, StringView text) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Write"
    "s the bytes of `text` at the file's position, as `File::write` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::write_text(ref"
    "<File, exclusive> f, StringView text) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Writes the byt"
    "es of `text` at the file's position, as `File::write` does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::seek\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::"
    "seek(ref<File, exclusive> f, u64 pos) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Moves the file's position to byte `p"
    "os` from the start.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::seek(ref<File, exclusive> f, u64 pos) : Result<void, FileError>"
    "\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Moves the file's position to byte `pos` from the start.\n\n    A position beyon"
    "d the end is allowed: reading there gives 0 bytes, and writing there extends the file.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}Fil"
    "e::len\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::len(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}The file's length in byt"
    "es.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::len(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The fil"
    "e's length in bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::create_new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::create_new(StringView path) : Res"
    "ult<Option<File>, FileError>\u{1f}Opens a new file at `path` for writing, only if nothing is there yet.\u{1f}\u{1f}"
    "export fn File::create_new(StringView path) : Result<Option<File>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    "
    "Opens a new file at `path` for writing, only if nothing is there yet.\n\n    `Ok(None)` when a file or"
    " a directory of that name already exists, and nothing is changed, so of two\n    programs that race t"
    "o make the same file exactly one wins: a lock file, a pid file, a file that must\n    never be overwr"
    "itten. `Err(NotFound)` when a directory above `path` does not exist; other errors as\n    `File::crea"
    "te`'s.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::create_private\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::create_private(StringView path) : Result<Op"
    "tion<File>, FileError>\u{1f}Opens a new file at `path` for writing that only its owner may read or write,"
    " only if nothing is there.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::create_private(StringView path) : Result<Option<File>, F"
    "ileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens a new file at `path` for writing that only its owner may read "
    "or write, only if nothing is there.\n\n    As `File::create_new`: `Ok(None)` when a file, a directory "
    "or a link of that name already exists, and\n    nothing is changed. On Unix the file is made with the"
    " mode 0600 (read and write for its owner alone),\n    whatever the umask; on Windows it takes its dir"
    "ectory's access list, which in the user's profile and\n    in `temp_dir()` admits the user alone. For"
    " a token, a key, a password or a session file; in a\n    directory other users may write, make one wi"
    "th `make_temp_dir` first.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::flush\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::flush(ref<File, exclusive> f) : R"
    "esult<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes everything written to the file so far durable.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::flush(r"
    "ef<File, exclusive> f) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes everything written to t"
    "he file so far durable.\n\n    The bytes are handed to the operating system and by it to the device, a"
    "nd from then on survive a crash\n    or a power loss; the write-then-rename pattern flushes before th"
    "e rename. `Err(Io)` on a file open only\n    for reading, or when the device fails.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::p"
    "osition\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::position(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}The byte the next "
    "read or write starts at, counted from the start of the file.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::position(ref<File, sha"
    "red> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The byte the next read or write starts at, co"
    "unted from the start of the file.\n\n    It is 0 when the file is opened (for `File::append`, the leng"
    "th once something is written) and is moved\n    by every read, `read_line`, write and `seek`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::"
    "fs\u{1f}File::close\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::close(File f) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Closes the file, first mak"
    "ing a file open for writing durable, and reports any failure.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::close(File f) : Resul"
    "t<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Closes the file, first making a file open for writing durab"
    "le, and reports any failure.\n\n    `Err(e)` when the environment reports a failure, including one for"
    " bytes written earlier; the file is\n    closed all the same. Destroying a `File` closes it too, but "
    "reports nothing.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::drop(ref<File, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Closes an o"
    "pen file when its `File` is destroyed, without making it durable or reporting a failure.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn "
    "File::drop(ref<File, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Closes an open file when its `File` is d"
    "estroyed, without making it durable or reporting a failure.\n\n    The destructor; programs do not cal"
    "l it, and call `File::close` to learn whether the bytes were stored.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}read_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export "
    "fn read_bytes(StringView path) : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\u{1f}The whole contents of the file at `path`"
    ", as bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_bytes(StringView path) : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n  "
    "  The whole contents of the file at `path`, as bytes.\n\n    The errors are `File::open`'s and `File::"
    "read_to_end`'s. Use `read_file` for text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}write_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn write_bytes(StringView "
    "path, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `data`"
    ", creating it or replacing its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn write_bytes(StringView path, slice<u8, shared> d"
    "ata) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `data`, c"
    "reating it or replacing its contents.\n\n    The errors are `write_file`'s, and a failed write (a full"
    " disk, an exceeded quota) is one of them. Use\n    `write_file` for text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}PathKind\u{1f}enum\u{1f}exp"
    "ort enum PathKind\u{1f}What a path names, as `path_kind` and `file_info` report it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum PathKind"
    "\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    What a path names, as `path_kind` and `file_info` report it.\n\n    - `File(n)`"
    ": a file of `n` bytes.\n    - `Dir`: a directory.\n    - `Other`: something else, such as a device or "
    "a socket.\n\n    Variants:\n        File(u64)\n        Dir\n        Other\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}make_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " make_dir(StringView path) : Result<bool, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the directory `path`, whose parent must e"
    "xist.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn make_dir(StringView path) : Result<bool, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the"
    " directory `path`, whose parent must exist.\n\n    `Ok(true)` when it was made; `Ok(false)` when a dir"
    "ectory was already there, so it is safe to call\n    again. `Err(NotFound)` when the parent does not "
    "exist; `Err(Io)` when something that is not a directory\n    is at `path`. Use `make_dir_all` to make"
    " the missing parents too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}remove_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remove_file(StringView path) : Result<v"
    "oid, FileError>\u{1f}Removes the file at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_file(StringView path) : Result<void, F"
    "ileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Removes the file at `path`.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when there is none."
    "\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}make_temp_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn make_temp_dir(StringView prefix) : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}M"
    "akes a new, empty directory under `temp_dir()` that only its owner may use, and returns its path.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn make_temp_dir(StringView prefix) : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes a "
    "new, empty directory under `temp_dir()` that only its owner may use, and returns its path.\n\n    The "
    "name is `prefix` followed by a version 4 UUID (`uuid_v4`, from the operating system's secure\n    sou"
    "rce); the directory is made only if nothing was at that name (another is tried if something was),\n  "
    "  so a directory, a file or a link another user made first is never used. On Unix its mode is 0700; "
    "on\n    Windows it takes the temporary directory's access list, which admits the user alone. Files ma"
    "de in it\n    with `File::create_new` or `write_file` are then out of other users' reach. Remove it w"
    "ith\n    `remove_dir_all` when done. `Err(NotFound)` when the temporary directory does not exist; fau"
    "lts\n    (`entropy_unavailable`) when the system has no secure source.\n\n    Example, \"make_temp_dir: "
    "a new directory each time\":\n        String a = Result::unwrap(make_temp_dir(\"std-test-\"));\n        S"
    "tring b = Result::unwrap(make_temp_dir(\"std-test-\"));\n        assert(a != b);\n        assert(Vec::le"
    "n(&Result::unwrap(list_dir(&a))) == 0);\n        assert(Result::is_ok(&remove_dir(&a)) && Result::is_"
    "ok(&remove_dir(&b)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}remove_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remove_dir(StringView path) : Result<void, F"
    "ileError>\u{1f}Removes the empty directory at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_dir(StringView path) : Result<voi"
    "d, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Removes the empty directory at `path`.\n\n    `Err(NotEmpty)` when"
    " it is not empty; `Err(NotFound)` when there is none. Use `remove_dir_all` to remove\n    a directory"
    " and everything in it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}rename\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn rename(StringView from, StringView to) : Result"
    "<void, FileError>\u{1f}Renames (moves) what is at `from` to `to`, replacing a file at `to`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn re"
    "name(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Renames (moves)"
    " what is at `from` to `to`, replacing a file at `to`.\n\n    Within one file system only: `Err(CrossDe"
    "vice)` when `to` is on another (a move from `/tmp` often is);\n    then `copy_file` and `remove_file`"
    ". `Err(InvalidName)` for a directory renamed into itself.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}list_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn list_dir(S"
    "tringView path) : Result<Vec<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The names of the entries of the directory `path`, s"
    "orted by their bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn list_dir(StringView path) : Result<Vec<String>, FileError>\n    in `"
    "std::fs`\n\n    The names of the entries of the directory `path`, sorted by their bytes.\n\n    The name"
    "s are entry names, not paths (join them to `path` with `path_join`), and never `.` or `..`.\n    `Err"
    "(Utf8(e))` for a name that is not UTF-8; `list_dir_bytes` lists such a directory too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}list"
    "_dir_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn list_dir_bytes(StringView path) : Result<Vec<Vec<u8>>, FileError>\u{1f}The names "
    "of the entries of the directory `path` as their bytes, sorted by them.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn list_dir_bytes(Str"
    "ingView path) : Result<Vec<Vec<u8>>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The names of the entries of th"
    "e directory `path` as their bytes, sorted by them.\n\n    `list_dir`, for a directory that may hold a "
    "name that is not UTF-8 (one from another system or an old\n    archive), which `list_dir` refuses for"
    " the whole directory: here every name is given, and\n    `String::from_utf8` tells the names a progra"
    "m can open from those it can only report (`to_hex`, or the\n    valid part before `Utf8Error::offset`"
    ") or skip. On Windows a name is its WTF-8 bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}read_link\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_link(StringVi"
    "ew path) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The target of the symbolic link at `path`, or `None` wh"
    "en what is at `path` is not a symbolic link.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_link(StringView path) : Result<Option<S"
    "tring>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The target of the symbolic link at `path`, or `None` when w"
    "hat is at `path` is not a symbolic link.\n\n    The link itself is looked at, not followed, so a direc"
    "tory walker skips links with it (or follows them\n    knowingly), and a link to itself or a missing t"
    "arget is no error. The target is the text the link\n    holds, relative to the link's directory unles"
    "s absolute; a byte sequence in it that is not UTF-8 is\n    replaced with U+FFFD. On Windows a juncti"
    "on counts as a link. `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at\n    `path`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_kind\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " path_kind(StringView path) : Result<PathKind, FileError>\u{1f}What `path` names: a file and its length, "
    "a directory, or something else.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_kind(StringView path) : Result<PathKind, FileError>\n"
    "    in `std::fs`\n\n    What `path` names: a file and its length, a directory, or something else.\n\n   "
    " A symbolic link is followed (`read_link` tells a link apart). `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `p"
    "ath`;\n    `Err(LinkLoop)` when links on it lead round in a loop.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_join\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn pa"
    "th_join(StringView base, StringView tail) : String\u{1f}The path `base` followed by `tail`, with the plat"
    "form's separator between them.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_join(StringView base, StringView tail) : String\n    i"
    "n `std::fs`\n\n    The path `base` followed by `tail`, with the platform's separator between them.\n\n  "
    "  No separator is added after one `base` already ends with. An absolute `tail` is returned alone. Re"
    "ads the\n    text only, never the file system.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_parent\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_parent(StringV"
    "iew p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The path `p` without its last part.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_parent(StringView p) : O"
    "ption<String>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The path `p` without its last part.\n\n    `None` for a root or th"
    "e empty path; the empty path for a single relative name. Reads the text only,\n    never the file sys"
    "tem; the grammar is the platform's (`\\` also separates parts on Windows).\n\n    Example, \"path_parent"
    ": a single name\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_parent(\"notes.txt\")) == \"\");\n        assert(Opt"
    "ion::is_none(&path_parent(\"\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_file_name\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_file_name(StringView p) "
    ": Option<String>\u{1f}The last part of the path `p`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_file_name(StringView p) : Option<Str"
    "ing>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The last part of the path `p`.\n\n    `None` when the last part is `..`, or"
    " when there is none (`/`, the empty path). Reads the text only.\n\n    Example, \"path_file_name: a fin"
    "al ..\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_file_name(\"src/main.cb\")) == \"main.cb\");\n        assert(O"
    "ption::is_none(&path_file_name(\"src/..\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_stem\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_stem(StringView p)"
    " : Option<String>\u{1f}The file name of `p` without its extension (`archive.tar` of `archive.tar.gz`).\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn path_stem(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The file name of `p` without"
    " its extension (`archive.tar` of `archive.tar.gz`).\n\n    A name whose only dot is its first byte has"
    " no extension, so its stem is the whole name (`.bashrc`).\n    Reads the text only.\n\n    Example, \"pa"
    "th_stem: the last dot\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_stem(\"archive.tar.gz\")) == \"archive.tar\")"
    ";\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_stem(\".bashrc\")) == \".bashrc\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_extension\u{1f}fn\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn path_extension(StringView p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The extension of the file name of `p`, without "
    "its dot (`gz` of `archive.tar.gz`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_extension(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in "
    "`std::fs`\n\n    The extension of the file name of `p`, without its dot (`gz` of `archive.tar.gz`).\n\n "
    "   `None` when there is none: a name with no dot, or whose only dot is its first byte. Reads the tex"
    "t only.\n\n    Example, \"path_extension: no extension\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_extension(\""
    "archive.tar.gz\")) == \"gz\");\n        assert(Option::is_none(&path_extension(\".bashrc\")));\n        ass"
    "ert(Option::is_none(&path_extension(\"README\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_is_absolute\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_is_abs"
    "olute(StringView p) : bool\u{1f}Whether `p` names a place without the working directory.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_"
    "is_absolute(StringView p) : bool\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Whether `p` names a place without the working"
    " directory.\n\n    By the platform's grammar: a leading `/` on Unix; `C:\\` or `\\\\server\\share\\` on Win"
    "dows. Reads the text\n    only.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_normalize\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_normalize(StringView p) : "
    "String\u{1f}The path `p` with every `.` part removed and every `x/..` (`x` a name) resolved.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn p"
    "ath_normalize(StringView p) : String\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The path `p` with every `.` part removed "
    "and every `x/..` (`x` a name) resolved.\n\n    By the text alone: a link is not followed, so the resul"
    "t can name another place than `p` when `x` is a\n    link. A leading `..` stays, `/..` is `/`, and a "
    "path with no part left is `.`. Use `path_canonical` to\n    ask the file system.\n\n    Example, \"path_"
    "normalize: . and ..\":\n        assert(path_normalize(\"a/./b/../c\") == path_join(\"a\", \"c\"));\n        a"
    "ssert(path_normalize(\"../x/..\") == \"..\");\n        assert(path_normalize(\"a/..\") == \".\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}p"
    "ath_canonical\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_canonical(StringView p) : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}The absolute pat"
    "h the operating system resolves `p` to, with every link followed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_canonical(StringVi"
    "ew p) : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The absolute path the operating system resol"
    "ves `p` to, with every link followed.\n\n    `p` must exist: `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `p`; `"
    "Err(Utf8(e))` when the result is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileInfo\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct FileInfo\u{1f}What `f"
    "ile_info` reports of a path.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct FileInfo\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    What `file_info` reports "
    "of a path.\n\n    - `kind`: what the path names, as `path_kind` gives it.\n    - `len`: the length in b"
    "ytes.\n    - `modified_unix`: the last modification, in whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z (rou"
    "nded down).\n    - `readonly`: whether the file is read-only.\n\n    Fields:\n        PathKind kind\n    "
    "    u64 len\n        i64 modified_unix\n        bool readonly\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}file_info\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn file_in"
    "fo(StringView path) : Result<FileInfo, FileError>\u{1f}The kind, length, modification time and read-only "
    "state of what is at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn file_info(StringView path) : Result<FileInfo, FileError>\n    "
    "in `std::fs`\n\n    The kind, length, modification time and read-only state of what is at `path`.\n\n   "
    " A symbolic link is followed, as `path_kind` follows it. `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `path`.\n"
    "\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}copy_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn copy_file(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}"
    "Copies the file `from` to `to`, replacing a file there, and returns the number of bytes copied.\u{1f}\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn copy_file(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<u64, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Copi"
    "es the file `from` to `to`, replacing a file there, and returns the number of bytes copied.\n\n    `Er"
    "r(SameFile)` when `to` is `from` itself (the same name written another way, or a link to it), and\n  "
    "  nothing is changed; `Err(IsDirectory)` when `from` is a directory. A source that is neither a file"
    " nor a\n    directory (a pipe such as `/dev/stdin` or a shell's `<(…)`, a device) is read to its en"
    "d and written to\n    `to`, as `cp` copies it; `to` then has the default permissions.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}copy_"
    "permissions\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn copy_permissions(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}"
    "Gives what is at `to` the permissions of what is at `from`: on Unix the mode bits, on Windows the re"
    "ad-only flag.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn copy_permissions(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\n"
    "    in `std::fs`\n\n    Gives what is at `to` the permissions of what is at `from`: on Unix the mode b"
    "its, on Windows the\n    read-only flag.\n\n    For the write-then-rename pattern: a file written besid"
    "e the original and renamed over it keeps the\n    original's permissions only when they are copied to"
    " it before the rename. `Err(NotFound)` when nothing\n    is at `from` or at `to`.\n\n    Example, \"copy"
    "_permissions: one file's permissions given to another\":\n        String tmp = temp_dir();\n        Str"
    "ing dir = path_join(&tmp, &sprintf(\"cb-perm-%v\", process_id()));\n        _ = make_dir_all(&dir);\n   "
    "     String a = path_join(&dir, \"a.txt\");\n        String b = path_join(&dir, \"b.txt\");\n        _ = w"
    "rite_file(&a, \"a\");\n        _ = write_file(&b, \"b\");\n        assert(Result::is_ok(&copy_permissions("
    "&a, &b)));\n        bool ra = Result::unwrap(file_info(&a)).readonly;\n        bool rb = Result::unwra"
    "p(file_info(&b)).readonly;\n        assert(ra == rb);\n        String gone = path_join(&dir, \"gone.txt"
    "\");\n        assert(Result::is_err(&copy_permissions(&gone, &b)));\n        _ = remove_dir_all(&dir);\n"
    "\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}make_dir_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn make_dir_all(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes th"
    "e directory `path` and every missing directory above it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn make_dir_all(StringView path) : "
    "Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the directory `path` and every missing directory"
    " above it.\n\n    `Ok(())` when they are all there already.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}remove_dir_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remo"
    "ve_dir_all(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Removes the directory `path` and everything in"
    " it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_dir_all(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Remo"
    "ves the directory `path` and everything in it.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `path`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std"
    "::env\u{1f}arg_bytes\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn arg_bytes(usize i, rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : is"
