    "sert(t == \"1267650600228229401496703205376\");\n        BigUint back = Result::unwrap(BigUint::parse(&"
    "t));\n        assert(BigUint::eq(&back, &big));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::math\u{1f}BigUint::parse\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn BigUint::pars"
    "e(StringView text) : Result<BigUint, ParseError>\u{1f}The number written in decimal digits in `text`.\u{1f}\u{1f}ex"
    "port fn BigUint::parse(StringView text) : Result<BigUint, ParseError>\n    in `std::math`\n\n    The nu"
    "mber written in decimal digits in `text`.\n\n    `Err(Empty)` when `text` is empty; `Err(Invalid(i))` "
    "at the first byte `i` that is not a digit (a sign is\n    not accepted).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}sum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export f"
    "n sum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : T\u{1f}The sum of `data`'s values in their own type; 0 for an e"
    "mpty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : T\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The sum of"
    " `data`'s values in their own type; 0 for an empty slice.\n\n    Added left to right with `+`, so an i"
    "nteger sum that does not fit in `T` faults (`arith_overflow`),\n    as `+` does. For the sum as an `f"
    "64` whatever `T` is, multiply `mean` by the count.\n\n    Example, \"sum: in the slice's own type\":\n   "
    "     array<i32, 4> a = [3, -1, 4, 1];\n        assert(sum(&a) == 7);\n        assert(sum(&a[0..0]) == "
    "0);\n        array<f64, 3> f = [0.5, 0.25, 2.0];\n        assert(sum(&f) == 2.75);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}mean\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn mean<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The arithmetic mean of `data`'s val"
    "ues; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn mean<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    "
    "in `std::stats`\n\n    The arithmetic mean of `data`'s values; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Each va"
    "lue is converted to `f64` with `to_float` and the values added left to right, then divided by\n    th"
    "e count. A NaN among the values makes the mean a NaN.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}variance\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn variance<T:"
    " number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The population variance of `data`'s values, the mean o"
    "f the squared deviations from their mean; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn variance<T: number>("
    "slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The population variance of `data`'s va"
    "lues, the mean of the squared deviations from their mean;\n    `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Two pa"
    "sses: the mean first, then the sum of `(x - mean)²` left to right, divided by the count. For\n    th"
    "e variance of a sample drawn from a larger population, use `sample_variance`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}stddev\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The population standard deviation "
    "of `data`'s values, the square root of `variance`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn stddev<T: n"
    "umber>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The population standard deviati"
    "on of `data`'s values, the square root of `variance`; `None` for an\n    empty slice.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}sa"
    "mple_variance\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sample_variance<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The sampl"
    "e variance of `data`'s values, the squared deviations from their mean divided by one less than the c"
    "ount (Bessel's correction); `None` for fewer than two values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sample_variance<T: number>("
    "slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The sample variance of `data`'s values"
    ", the squared deviations from their mean divided by one less\n    than the count (Bessel's correction"
    "); `None` for fewer than two values.\n\n    For values that are the whole population, use `variance`.\n"
    "\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}sample_stddev\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sample_stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64"
    ">\u{1f}The sample standard deviation of `data`'s values, the square root of `sample_variance`; `None` for"
    " fewer than two values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sample_stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n   "
    " in `std::stats`\n\n    The sample standard deviation of `data`'s values, the square root of `sample_v"
    "ariance`; `None` for\n    fewer than two values.\n\n    Example, \"variance: population and sample\":\n   "
    "     array<u32, 8> a = [2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(variance(&a)) == 4.0)"
    ";\n        assert(Option::unwrap(stddev(&a)) == 2.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(sample_variance(&"
    "a)) == 32.0 / 7.0);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&variance(&a[0..0])));\n        assert(Option::unw"
    "rap(variance(&a[0..1])) == 0.0);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&sample_variance(&a[0..1])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":stats\u{1f}median\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn median<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The median of `dat"
    "a`'s values: the middle one of them sorted, or the mean of the two middle ones when the count is eve"
    "n; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn median<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    "
    "in `std::stats`\n\n    The median of `data`'s values: the middle one of them sorted, or the mean of th"
    "e two middle ones\n    when the count is even; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    The values are copied,"
    " converted to `f64` and sorted; `data` is not changed. A NaN is passed over, as\n    `minimum` and `m"
    "aximum` pass it over: the median is that of the other values, and a NaN only when every\n    value is"
    " one.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}percentile\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn percentile<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data, f64 p) : Opt"
    "ion<f64>\u{1f}The `p`th percentile of `data`'s values, `p` from 0 to 100, with linear interpolation betwe"
    "en the two sorted values it falls between; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn percentile<T: numbe"
    "r>(slice<T, shared> data, f64 p) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n    requires\n        p >= 0.0 && "
    "p <= 100.0, assert_failed, \"percentile: `p` is outside 0 to 100\"\n\n    The `p`th percentile of `data`"
    "'s values, `p` from 0 to 100, with linear interpolation between the\n    two sorted values it falls b"
    "etween; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    The position is `(count - 1) · p / 100` in the sorted value"
    "s (R's type 7, the default of spreadsheets\n    and NumPy): `percentile(data, 0.0)` is the minimum, `"
    "percentile(data, 100.0)` the maximum, and\n    `percentile(data, 50.0)` the median. A NaN among the v"
    "alues is passed over, as those three pass it\n    over (the result is a NaN only when every value is "
    "one). Faults (`assert_failed`) when `p` is below 0,\n    above 100 or a NaN.\n\n    Example, \"percentil"
    "e: interpolated between neighbours\":\n        array<i64, 5> a = [15, 20, 35, 40, 50];\n        assert("
    "Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 25.0)) == 20.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 40.0)) ="
    "= 29.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 50.0)) == 35.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap"
    "(median(&a)) == 35.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(median(&a[0..4])) == 27.5);\n        assert(Opti"
    "on::unwrap(percentile(&a, 0.0)) == 15.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 100.0)) == 50"
    ".0);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&median(&a[0..0])));\n\n    Example, \"median and percentile: a NaN"
    " passed over\":\n        f64 nan = 0.0 / 0.0;\n        array<f64, 4> d = [3.0, nan, 1.0, 2.0];\n        "
    "assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&d, 100.0)) == 3.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&d, 0"
    ".0)) == 1.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(median(&d)) == 2.0);\n        array<f64, 2> all = [nan, n"
    "an];\n        f64 m = Option::unwrap(median(&all));\n        assert(m != m);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}minimum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn minimum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The smallest of `data`'s values as "
    "an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn minimum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f"
    "64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The smallest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice."
    "\n\n    A NaN is passed over: the result is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}maximum\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The largest of `data`'s values a"
    "s an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option"
    "<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The largest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice"
    ".\n\n    A NaN is passed over: the result is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\n    Example, \"minimu"
    "m and maximum: as f64, a NaN passed over\":\n        array<i16, 4> a = [7, -3, 12, 0];\n        assert("
    "Option::unwrap(minimum(&a)) == -3.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(maximum(&a)) == 12.0);\n        f"
    "64 nan = 0.0 / 0.0;\n        array<f64, 3> f = [nan, 2.5, 1.5];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(minimum"
    "(&f)) == 1.5);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(maximum(&f)) == 2.5);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&m"
    "inimum(&f[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}Summary\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Summary\u{1f}What `describe` gives: the cou"
    "nt of the values and their minimum, maximum, mean, population standard deviation and median.\u{1f}\u{1f}export"
    " struct Summary\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    What `describe` gives: the count of the values and their mi"
    "nimum, maximum, mean, population standard\n    deviation and median.\n\n    Fields:\n        usize count"
    "\n        f64 minimum\n        f64 maximum\n        f64 mean\n        f64 stddev\n        f64 median\n\u{1f}\u{1e}st"
    "d::stats\u{1f}describe\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn describe<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<Summary>\u{1f}The coun"
    "t, `minimum`, `maximum`, `mean`, `stddev` and `median` of `data`'s values as one `Summary`; `None` f"
    "or an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn describe<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<Summary>\n    in `s"
    "td::stats`\n\n    The count, `minimum`, `maximum`, `mean`, `stddev` and `median` of `data`'s values as"
    " one `Summary`;\n    `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Example, \"describe: a run of timings\":\n        a"
    "rray<u32, 6> ms = [12, 15, 11, 40, 13, 14];\n        Summary s = Option::unwrap(describe(&ms));\n     "
    "   assert(s.count == 6 && s.minimum == 11.0 && s.maximum == 40.0);\n        assert(s.mean == 17.5 && "
    "s.median == 13.5);\n        assert(s.stddev == sqrt(Option::unwrap(variance(&ms))));\n        assert(O"
    "ption::is_none(&describe(&ms[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct DateTime\u{1f}A moment i"
    "n UTC: a date of the proleptic Gregorian calendar and a time of day to the second.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Da"
    "teTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A moment in UTC: a date of the proleptic Gregorian calendar and a tim"
    "e of day to the second.\n\n    `year` 0 is 1 BC and -1 is 2 BC; there are no leap seconds, so every da"
    "y has 86,400 seconds. Any field\n    values can be written in a struct literal: `DateTime::new` check"
    "s them, and `DateTime::to_unix` faults\n    on a value that names no moment. Keep moments in UTC and "
    "convert with `DateTime::to_local` only for\n    display.\n\n    Fields:\n        i64 year\n        u8 mon"
    "th\n        u8 day\n        u8 hour\n        u8 minute\n        u8 second\n\n    Example, \"DateTime: a neg"
    "ative year\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime { .year = -1, .month = 3, .day = 1, .hour = 9, .minute = "
    "5, .second = 0 };\n        String s = DateTime::to_iso(&t);\n        assert(s == \"-0001-03-01T09:05:00"
    "Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum Weekday\u{1f}A day of the week, `Monday` to `Sunday`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export e"
    "num Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A day of the week, `Monday` to `Sunday`.\n\n    Variants:\n        "
    "Monday\n        Tuesday\n        Wednesday\n        Thursday\n        Friday\n        Saturday\n        Su"
    "nday\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_unix(i64 s) : DateTime\u{1f}The moment `"
    "s` seconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or before it when `s` is negative.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_"
    "unix(i64 s) : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `s` seconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or"
    " before it when `s` is negative.\n\n    Every `i64` names a moment, so this never fails.\n\n    Example,"
    " \"DateTime::from_unix: before 1970\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_unix(-1);\n        assert(t."
    "year == 1969 && t.month == 12 && t.day == 31);\n        assert(t.hour == 23 && t.minute == 59 && t.se"
    "cond == 59);\n        assert(DateTime::to_unix(&t) == -1);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::now\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Dat"
    "eTime::now() : DateTime\u{1f}The moment now by the wall clock, to the second: `DateTime::from_unix(unix_s"
    "econds())`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::now() : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment now by the wall"
    " clock, to the second: `DateTime::from_unix(unix_seconds())`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " DateTime::new(i64 year, u8 month, u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : Option<DateTime>\u{1f}The `Da"
    "teTime` with these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::new(i64 year, u8"
    " month, u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : Option<DateTime>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The `DateT"
    "ime` with these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\n\n    `None` for a month 13, a 30 Februar"
    "y, a 29 February in a common year, an hour 24 or a second 60. A leap\n    year is divisible by 4 and "
    "not by 100, or by 400.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::new: 29 February\":\n        Option<DateTime> leap = D"
    "ateTime::new(2024, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0);\n        Option<DateTime> common = DateTime::new(2023, 2, 29, 0, "
    "0, 0);\n        assert(Option::is_some(&leap));\n        assert(Option::is_none(&common));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time"
    "\u{1f}DateTime::to_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : i64\u{1f}The seconds from 19"
    "70-01-01T00:00:00Z to the moment `t`, negative before it; the inverse of `from_unix`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Dat"
    "eTime::to_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n    requires\n        DateTime::vali"
    "d(t), invalid_datetime\n\n    The seconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to the moment `t`, negative before"
    " it; the inverse of `from_unix`.\n\n    Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when the fields of `t` name no mom"
    "ent, or when the count does not fit in an\n    `i64`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::weekday\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Date"
    "Time::weekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\u{1f}The day of the week of the date of `t`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn "
    "DateTime::weekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The day of the week of "
    "the date of `t`.\n\n    Defined for every valid `t`, however far from 1970. Faults (`invalid_datetime`"
    ") when the date of `t`\n    names no day.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::weekday: 1970-01-01\":\n        Date"
    "Time t = DateTime::from_unix(0);\n        Weekday w = DateTime::weekday(&t);\n        assert(Weekday::"
    "text(&w) == \"Thursday\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weekday::text(ref<Weekday, shared> w"
    ") : str\u{1f}The day's English name (`Monday`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weekday::text(ref<Weekday, shared> w) : str\n  "
    "  in `std::time`\n\n    The day's English name (`Monday`).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn D"
    "ateTime::to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment in ISO 8601 form in UTC, such as `2026-"
    "10-03T10:17:00Z`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\n    in `std::time`\n\n"
    "    The moment in ISO 8601 form in UTC, such as `2026-10-03T10:17:00Z`.\n\n    Each field is zero-padd"
    "ed to its width. A year outside 0 ..= 9999 is written with its sign and at least\n    four digits (`-"
    "0001`, `+10000`). Use `iso_from_unix_ms` for a timestamp with milliseconds.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::fr"
    "om_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_iso(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\u{1f}Reads a momen"
    "t in ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 form (`to_iso`'s, `to_iso_local`'s), or a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`) as its "
    "midnight.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_iso(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `std:"
    ":time`\n\n    Reads a moment in ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 form (`to_iso`'s, `to_iso_local`'s), or a date alo"
    "ne (`YYYY-MM-DD`)\n    as its midnight.\n\n    The year is four digits, or a sign and four or more. The"
    " date and the time are parted by `T` (or `t`, or a\n    space, as PostgreSQL writes them), and the ti"
    "me is `HH:MM:SS`. The seconds may carry a fraction\n    (`:37.25Z`), which is dropped; `unix_ms_from_"
    "iso` keeps it to the millisecond. The zone is `Z` (or `z`),\n    an offset from UTC, `+HH:MM`, `-HH:M"
    "M`, `+HH` or `+HHMM`, by which the moment is converted to UTC\n    (`2024-03-10T03:30:00-04:00` is `2"
    "024-03-10T07:30:00Z`), or nothing: a time without a zone is read as its\n    fields as written (a `Da"
    "teTime` knows no zone; `std::time` takes it as UTC). No other form is read.\n\n    - `Err(Empty)` for "
    "the empty text.\n    - `Err(Invalid(i))`: byte `i` is the first that does not fit the form (the lengt"
    "h when the text stops\n      short).\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the fields name no moment (a month 13, "
    "a 29 February in a common year, a second\n      60, an offset of 24 hours or more or with a minute pa"
    "st 59), or the moment is too far from 1970 for `to_unix` (its seconds do not fit in an `i64`), so a\n"
    "      `DateTime` read here can always be converted.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_iso: a date alone,"
    " a short time\":\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2026-10-03\"))\n        {\n            Ok(t)  : asse"
    "rt(t.day == 3 && t.hour == 0),\n            Err(_) : assert(false),\n        }\n        match (DateTime"
    "::from_iso(\"2026-10-03T10:17\"))\n        {\n            Err(Invalid(i)) : assert(i == 16),\n           "
    " _               : assert(false),\n        }\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_iso: offsets and a space f"
    "or the T\":\n        String z = DateTime::to_iso(&Result::unwrap(DateTime::from_iso(\"2024-03-10T03:30:"
    "00-04:00\")));\n        assert(z == \"2024-03-10T07:30:00Z\");\n        z = DateTime::to_iso(&Result::unw"
    "rap(DateTime::from_iso(\"2023-11-15 09:13:20+11\")));\n        assert(z == \"2023-11-14T22:13:20Z\");\n   "
    "     z = DateTime::to_iso(&Result::unwrap(DateTime::from_iso(\"2023-11-14 22:13:20\")));\n        asser"
    "t(z == \"2023-11-14T22:13:20Z\");\n        z = DateTime::to_iso(&Result::unwrap(DateTime::from_iso(\"202"
    "0-01-01T00:10:00+0530\")));\n        assert(z == \"2019-12-31T18:40:00Z\");\n        assert(unix_ms_from_"
    "iso(\"2023-11-14 22:13:20.5+00\") == Ok(1700000000500));\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2024-03-10"
    "T03:30:00+24:00\"))\n        {\n            Err(OutOfRange) : {},\n            _               : assert("
    "false),\n        }\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2024-03-10T03:30:00+4\"))\n        {\n            "
    "Err(Invalid(i)) : assert(i == 21),\n            _               : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::tim"
    "e\u{1f}DateTime::eq\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Wh"
    "ether `a` and `b` are the same moment, every field equal.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<DateTime, shar"
    "ed> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Whether `a` and `b` are the same mome"
    "nt, every field equal.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::less\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::less(ref<DateTime, shared> "
    "a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` is earlier than `b`, comparing the fields in order of"
    " significance.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::less(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\n  "
    "  in `std::time`\n\n    Whether `a` is earlier than `b`, comparing the fields in order of significance"
    ".\n\n    For valid values this is the order of `to_unix`. It fits `Vec::sort_by`.\n\n    Example, \"DateT"
    "ime::less: ordering\":\n        DateTime a = DateTime::from_unix(100);\n        DateTime b = DateTime::"
    "from_unix(160);\n        assert(DateTime::less(&a, &b));\n        assert(!DateTime::less(&b, &a));\n   "
    "     assert(!DateTime::eq(&a, &b));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\u{1f}The wall clock "
    "in milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, rounded down.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\n    in `std::ti"
    "me`\n\n    The wall clock in milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, rounded down.\n\n    `unix_ms() / "
    "1000` is `unix_seconds()` when read together. The wall clock may be changed while the\n    program ru"
    "ns; measure elapsed time with `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}local_offset_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn local_of"
    "fset_seconds(i64 unix) : i64\u{1f}How many seconds the machine's local time is ahead of UTC at the moment"
    " `unix` seconds after 1970.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn local_offset_seconds(i64 unix) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    "
    "How many seconds the machine's local time is ahead of UTC at the moment `unix` seconds after 1970.\n\n"
    "    Negative when local time is behind UTC; daylight saving included, by the operating system's rule"
    " for the\n    zone the program runs in. Between -64800 and 64800. For a moment the system has no rule"
    " for, the offset\n    now. It differs between machines, and on one machine between moments.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::ti"
    "me\u{1f}DateTime::to_local\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_local(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : DateTime\u{1f}The moment"
    " `t`, given in UTC, written in the machine's local time.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_local(ref<DateTime,"
    " shared> t) : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `t`, given in UTC, written in the machine'"
    "s local time.\n\n    The result is a `DateTime` like any other and knows nothing of its zone: print it"
    "s fields, not `to_iso`\n    (which writes `Z`; `DateTime::to_iso_local` writes a local time with its "
    "offset), and keep the UTC value\n    for arithmetic, since `to_unix` of the result is the moment plus"
    " the offset. Faults (`invalid_datetime`)\n    when `t` names no moment, or when the moment is within "
    "a day of an `i64` limit.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_iso_local\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso_local(ref<"
    "DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment `t`, given in UTC, in ISO 8601 form in the machine's local "
    "time with its offset from UTC.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso_local(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\n "
    "   in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `t`, given in UTC, in ISO 8601 form in the machine's local time wi"
    "th its offset from UTC.\n\n    Such as `2024-03-10T03:30:00-04:00` for `2024-03-10T07:30:00Z` in New Y"
    "ork; `+00:00` where local time is\n    UTC. The text names the same moment as `to_iso`'s, so `DateTim"
    "e::from_iso` and any other reader of ISO 8601\n    get `t` back (an offset\n    that is not a whole nu"
    "mber of minutes, which no zone has used since 1972, is cut to the minute, and the\n    time with it)."
    " The offset is `local_offset_seconds`'s at that moment. Faults as `DateTime::to_local`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}"
    "monotonic_ns\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn monotonic_ns() : u64\u{1f}Nanoseconds on a monotonic clock, from a start fixed "
    "for the whole program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn monotonic_ns() : u64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Nanoseconds on a mono"
    "tonic clock, from a start fixed for the whole program.\n\n    The clock never goes backwards, so the d"
    "ifference of two readings measures how long something took. Its\n    values mean nothing else; use `u"
    "nix_ms` for the time of day.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_seconds() : i64\u{1f}The wall clo"
    "ck: whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_seconds() : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n  "
    "  The wall clock: whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.\n\n    The wall clock may be changed while"
    " the program runs; measure elapsed time with `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}sleep_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sleep"
    "_ms(u64 ms)\u{1f}Makes the calling thread do nothing for at least `ms` milliseconds; other threads run me"
    "anwhile.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sleep_ms(u64 ms)\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Makes the calling thread do nothing for"
    " at least `ms` milliseconds; other threads run meanwhile.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}iso_from_unix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn "
    "iso_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\u{1f}The moment `ms` milliseconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z in `to_iso"
    "`'s form, with milliseconds.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn iso_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Th"
    "e moment `ms` milliseconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z in `to_iso`'s form, with milliseconds.\n\n    Th"
    "e form is `2026-10-07T12:34:56.789Z`, a log line's or a JSON document's timestamp. A negative `ms`\n "
    "   is before 1970, the milliseconds still counted forward within their second.\n\n    Example, \"iso_fr"
    "om_unix_ms: before 1970\":\n        assert(iso_from_unix_ms(-1) == \"1969-12-31T23:59:59.999Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":time\u{1f}unix_ms_from_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_ms_from_iso(StringView text) : Result<i64, ParseError>\u{1f}The "
    "milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of a moment in `DateTime::from_iso`'s form.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_"
    "ms_from_iso(StringView text) : Result<i64, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The milliseconds sinc"
    "e 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of a moment in `DateTime::from_iso`'s form.\n\n    A fraction of the seconds is"
    " kept to the millisecond: its first three digits, fewer filled with zeros,\n    further digits ignore"
    "d (`.5` is 500, `.1234` is 123). The errors are `from_iso`'s; `Err(OutOfRange)`\n    also when the mi"
    "lliseconds do not fit in an `i64`.\n\n    Example, \"unix_ms_from_iso: fractions\":\n        assert(unix_"
    "ms_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:00:01.5Z\") == Ok(1500));\n        assert(unix_ms_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:0"
    "0:00.1239Z\") == Ok(123));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_http_date\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_http_date(ref"
    "<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment in HTTP's date form, such as `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GM"
    "T`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_http_date(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\n    in `std::time`\n    requi"
    "res\n        t.year >= 0 && t.year <= 9999, invalid_datetime\n\n    The moment in HTTP's date form, suc"
    "h as `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`.\n\n    The form (RFC 9110 §5.6.7, the IMF-fixdate) is what the "
    "`Date`, `Last-Modified` and `Expires` headers\n    carry. Faults (`invalid_datetime`) for a year outs"
    "ide 0 ..= 9999, or fields that name no moment.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::to_http_date: the IMF-fixdat"
    "e\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_unix(784111777);\n        assert(DateTime::to_http_date(&t) ="
    "= \"Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_http_date\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from"
    "_http_date(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\u{1f}Reads an HTTP date in any of the three fo"
    "rms a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5.6.7).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_http_date(StringView text)"
    " : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Reads an HTTP date in any of the three forms"
    " a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5.6.7).\n\n    The forms are `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT` (the"
    " one to send), the obsolete RFC 850\n    `Sunday, 06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT` (a two-digit year: 70 to 99"
    " are 1970 to 1999, 00 to 69 are 2000 to\n    2069) and asctime's `Sun Nov  6 08:49:37 1994`. The word"
    "s are separated by ASCII whitespace; the weekday\n    is not checked against the date.\n\n    - `Err(Em"
    "pty)` when the text has no words.\n    - `Err(Invalid(i))`: `i` is the start of the first word that d"
    "oes not fit (a zone other than `GMT`, a word\n      after the last), or the text's length when it sto"
    "ps short.\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the fields name no moment.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_http_dat"
    "e: a two-digit year\":\n        match (DateTime::from_http_date(\"Sunday, 06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT\"))\n   "
    "     {\n            Ok(t)  : assert(t.year == 1994 && t.month == 11 && t.day == 6),\n            Err(_"
    ") : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Command\u{1f}A program to run: i"
    "ts name or path, its arguments, its working directory and its environment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Command\n  "
    "  in `std::process`\n\n    A program to run: its name or path, its arguments, its working directory an"
    "d its environment.\n\n    Made by `Command::new`, changed by `arg`, `current_dir`, `env` and `clear_en"
    "v`, and run by `output`,\n    `output_with_input`, `status` or `spawn`, any number of times. No shell"
    " is involved: each argument\n    reaches the program exactly as given, with no splitting, quoting or "
    "expansion; a program that wants a\n    shell runs one by name (`/bin/sh` with `-c`).\n\n    Running fai"
    "ls with `Err(NotFound)` when there is no such program or no such working directory,\n    `Err(Denied)"
    "` when it may not be run, and `Err(Io)` for any other failure to start it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Output\u{1f}str"
    "uct\u{1f}export struct Output\u{1f}How a run of `Command::output` ended, and everything the program wrote.\u{1f}\u{1f}ex"
    "port struct Output\n    in `std::process`\n\n    How a run of `Command::output` ended, and everything t"
    "he program wrote.\n\n    - `status`: the exit code, or on Unix minus the number of the signal that end"
    "ed it (`-9` for SIGKILL).\n    - `stdout`: every byte the program wrote to its standard output.\n    -"
    " `stderr`: every byte the program wrote to its standard error.\n\n    Fields:\n        i32 status\n     "
    "   Vec<u8> stdout\n        Vec<u8> stderr\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::new(Strin"
    "gView program) : Command\u{1f}A command running `program` with no arguments, in this program's working di"
    "rectory and environment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::new(StringView program) : Command\n    in `std::process`"
    "\n\n    A command running `program` with no arguments, in this program's working directory and environ"
    "ment.\n\n    A name without a separator is looked for on `PATH`, as the system does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Co"
    "mmand::arg\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView a)\u{1f}Appends one argument, "
    "passed to the program exactly as given.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringVie"
    "w a)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Appends one argument, passed to the program exactly as given.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":process\u{1f}Command::current_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringVie"
    "w dir)\u{1f}Sets the working directory the program starts in.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<Command"
    ", exclusive> c, StringView dir)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Sets the working directory the program st"
    "arts in.\n\n    A directory that does not exist makes the run fail with `Err(NotFound)`, as a missing "
    "program does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::env(ref<Command, exclusive> c, Strin"
    "gView name, StringView value)\u{1f}Sets the environment variable `name` to `value` for the program.\u{1f}\u{1f}expo"
    "rt fn Command::env(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView name, StringView value)\n    in `std::proces"
    "s`\n\n    Sets the environment variable `name` to `value` for the program.\n\n    The variables set this"
    " way go on top of the inherited environment, or of an empty one after\n    `Command::clear_env`. A na"
    "me that is empty or holds `=`, or a NUL in the name or the value, makes a run\n    fail with `Err(Io)"
    "`: such a name would set another variable in the child.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::clear_env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export "
    "fn Command::clear_env(ref<Command, exclusive> c)\u{1f}Makes the program start with no environment variabl"
    "es but those `Command::env` sets.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::clear_env(ref<Command, exclusive> c)\n    in `s"
    "td::process`\n\n    Makes the program start with no environment variables but those `Command::env` set"
    "s.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Outp"
    "ut, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program to its end with an empty standard input, and gives its status and ev"
    "erything it wrote.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Output, FileError>\n  "
    "  in `std::process`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with an empty standard input, and gives its sta"
    "tus and everything it wrote.\n\n    Both streams are read at once, so neither pipe fills and stops the"
    " program.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::output_with_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output_with_input(ref<Comm"
    "and, shared> c, slice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program to its end wit"
    "h `input` as its standard input, as `Command::output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output_with_input(re"
    "f<Command, shared> c, slice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n  "
    "  Runs the program to its end with `input` as its standard input, as `Command::output` does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::"
    "process\u{1f}Command::status\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<i32, FileError"
    ">\u{1f}Runs the program to its end with this program's standard input, output and error, and gives its st"
    "atus.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<i32, FileError>\n    in `std::proce"
    "ss`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with this program's standard input, output and error, and gives"
    " its status.\n\n    The status is `Output::status`'s: the exit code, or on Unix minus the signal that "
    "ended it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Child\u{1f}A running child process, its stan"
    "dard input, output and error each a pipe to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Child\n    in `std:"
    ":process`\n\n    A running child process, its standard input, output and error each a pipe to this pro"
    "gram.\n\n    Made by `Command::spawn`. Destroying a `Child` closes its pipes but does not end it: a ru"
    "nning child\n    runs on. On Unix a destroyed child is still reaped, at once when it was killed and o"
    "therwise once it\n    has ended (by the next start or destruction of a child), so it leaves no zombie"
    ". A child that fills a\n    pipe nobody reads stops until it is read, so read its output before `Chil"
    "d::wait`, or use\n    `Command::output`. `Child::take_input` and `Child::take_output` move its pipes "
    "out, for use from other\n    threads.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::spawn\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::spawn(ref<C"
    "ommand, shared> c) : Result<Child, FileError>\u{1f}Starts the program and returns at once, its three stre"
    "ams pipes to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::spawn(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Child, FileErr"
    "or>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Starts the program and returns at once, its three streams pipes to th"
    "is program.\n\n    A pipe holds only so much (64 KiB on Linux): a child that writes more stops until t"
    "his program reads\n    it, so read its output (`Child::read_output`, `read_output_line`, or `Child::t"
    "ake_output` in another\n    thread) before `Child::wait`, or use `Command::output`, which reads both "
    "streams while it waits.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::id\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::id(ref<Child, shared> ch) : u64"
    "\u{1f}The operating system's number for the child (its process id).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::id(ref<Child, share"
    "d> ch) : u64\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The operating system's number for the child (its process id)"
    ".\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::write_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::write_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, slice<"
    "u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Writes all of `data` to the child's standard input.\u{1f}\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn Child::write_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError"
    ">\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Writes all of `data` to the child's standard input.\n\n    `Err(Io)` when"
    " the child has ended or closed its input; it never ends this program. Does nothing once\n    `Child::"
    "take_input` has taken the input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::close_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::close_input(r"
    "ef<Child, exclusive> ch)\u{1f}Closes the child's standard input, so that the child reads its end.\u{1f}\u{1f}export"
    " fn Child::close_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Closes the child's stand"
    "ard input, so that the child reads its end.\n\n    Does nothing once `Child::take_input` has taken the"
    " input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, "
    "ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends to `buf` what has arrived"
    " on the child's standard output, up to `max` bytes, and returns how many.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_out"
    "put(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n   "
    " in `std::process`\n\n    Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard output, up to `max"
    "` bytes, and returns how many.\n\n    Waits for at least one byte; at most a MiB is read at a time. `O"
    "k(0)` (for a `max` above 0) means the\n    end of the output.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_output_line\u{1f}"
    "fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}T"
    "he next line of the child's standard output, without its `'\\n'` or a `'\\r'` just before it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export "
    "fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std"
    "::process`\n\n    The next line of the child's standard output, without its `'\\n'` or a `'\\r'` just be"
    "fore it.\n\n    `Ok(None)` at the end of the output; `Err(Utf8(e))` for a line that is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}st"
    "d::process\u{1f}Child::read_error\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, e"
    "xclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child'"
    "s standard error, as `Child::read_output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error(ref<Child, exclusive> c"
    "h, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    App"
    "ends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output` does.\n\n    Wha"
    "t `Child::read_error_line` has read ahead comes first.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_error_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expo"
    "rt fn Child::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next "
    "line of the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output_line` reads standard output.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn C"
    "hild::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::pro"
    "cess`\n\n    The next line of the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output_line` reads standard "
    "output.\n\n    Each stream is read ahead on its own.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::wait\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::wa"
    "it(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<i32, FileError>\u{1f}Closes the child's standard input, waits for t"
    "he child to end and gives its status.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::wait(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<i32,"
    " FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Closes the child's standard input, waits for the child to end"
    " and gives its status.\n\n    The status is `Output::status`'s. Asked again, it gives the same status."
    " It does not read the child's\n    output: a child that fills its output pipe before it ends waits fo"
