    "ll\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::kill(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Ends the child at on"
    "ce (SIGKILL on Unix, TerminateProcess on Windows).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::kill(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) "
    ": Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Ends the child at once (SIGKILL on Unix, Termin"
    "ateProcess on Windows).\n\n    `Child::wait` then gives its status. Nothing happens to a child that ha"
    "s already ended. Use\n    `Child::terminate` to let the child stop cleanly, and `Child::kill_tree` to"
    " end the processes it started\n    too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::kill_tree\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::kill_tree"
    "(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Ends the child and every process it started, an"
    "d theirs in turn.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::kill_tree(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\n  "
    "  in `std::process`\n\n    Ends the child and every process it started, and theirs in turn.\n\n    On Li"
    "nux the processes are found from `/proc`; on Windows by `taskkill /T`; elsewhere only the child is\n "
    "   ended, as `Child::kill` ends it. `Child::wait` then gives its status. A process that has left the"
    " tree\n    before the call is not reached: one that forked twice to become a daemon now has init as i"
    "ts parent,\n    and nothing links it to the child.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::terminate\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child"
    "::terminate(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Asks the child to end: SIGTERM on Un"
    "ix, which a program may catch to stop cleanly.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::terminate(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)"
    " : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Asks the child to end: SIGTERM on Unix, which "
    "a program may catch to stop cleanly.\n\n    On Windows it ends the child as `Child::kill` does. On Uni"
    "x the status `Child::wait` then gives is -15,\n    unless the child handles the signal. Nothing happe"
    "ns, and `Ok(())` is returned, for a child that has\n    already ended.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}"
    "export fn Child::drop(ref<Child, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Closes the child's pipes when its `Child` is destr"
    "oyed, leaving the child running.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::drop(ref<Child, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::pro"
    "cess`\n\n    Closes the child's pipes when its `Child` is destroyed, leaving the child running.\n\n    T"
    "he destructor; programs do not call it. On Unix the process is reaped all the same: at once when it "
    "was\n    killed (`Child::kill`, `kill_tree`), otherwise once it has ended, when the next child is sta"
    "rted or\n    destroyed, so it leaves no zombie.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}ChildInput\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource stru"
    "ct ChildInput\u{1f}A child's standard input as a value of its own, taken out of its `Child` by `Child::ta"
    "ke_input`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct ChildInput\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A child's standard input as"
    " a value of its own, taken out of its `Child` by `Child::take_input`.\n\n    It is independent of the "
    "`Child` and of the child's output, and outlives the `Child`, so one thread can\n    write a child's i"
    "nput while another reads its output (or one child's output can feed another's input).\n    Destroying"
    " it closes the pipe, and the child reading its input then finds its end.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}ChildOutput\u{1f}"
    "struct\u{1f}export resource struct ChildOutput\u{1f}A child's standard output as a value of its own, taken out"
    " of its `Child` by `Child::take_output`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct ChildOutput\n    in `std::process`\n\n"
    "    A child's standard output as a value of its own, taken out of its `Child` by `Child::take_output"
    "`.\n\n    It is independent of the `Child` and of the child's input, and outlives the `Child`. Destroy"
    "ing it closes\n    the pipe.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::take_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::take_input(ref<Chil"
    "d, exclusive> ch) : Option<ChildInput>\u{1f}The child's standard input, moved out of the `Child`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export"
    " fn Child::take_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Option<ChildInput>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The "
